# PART I — UNDERSTAND THE INITIATIVE

## 1. Official identity

Project name: Math Lab

Tagline:

**Explore mathematics. Investigate questions. Learn together.**

Official contact email: mathlab.official12@gmail.com

Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/mathlabofficial_/

Math Lab is a student-centered initiative built around a simple idea: mathematics is more than a subject to study. It is something to investigate, question, and discover together.

Through collaborative mathematical investigations and locally organized learning communities, Math Lab aims to give students opportunities to engage more deeply with mathematics, develop their reasoning skills, and participate in a wider community of mathematical exploration.

Math Lab is not simply a collection of mathematical calculators, formula sheets, or routine practice exercises. Digital tools can support its mission, but the initiative's central purpose is to create meaningful opportunities for mathematical investigation and collaboration.

The software must reflect this distinction.

## 2. Why Math Lab exists

Mathematics education can become overly focused on memorizing formulas, following procedures, and arriving at correct answers. These skills have their place, but they are only part of mathematics.

Mathematics is also about:

- Recognizing patterns.
- Asking productive questions.
- Formulating and testing conjectures.
- Constructing proofs.
- Investigating unfamiliar problems.
- Comparing alternative approaches.
- Understanding why a result is true.
- Identifying counterexamples.
- Communicating mathematical arguments.
- Learning from unsuccessful approaches.
- Building on the ideas of other learners.

Math Lab exists to help students experience this side of mathematics.

The platform should encourage learners to move beyond passively receiving information and toward actively investigating mathematical ideas.

It should also support participation beyond a single classroom, school, or community.

The long-term goal is a community in which mathematical curiosity can develop into meaningful, sustained participation.

## 3. Core programs

### A. National Investigations

National Investigations are a central part of Math Lab's developing program.

They provide shared mathematical investigations that students can explore through participating Local Labs.

An investigation should not be merely a worksheet with a collection of unrelated questions. It should have a coherent mathematical theme, a meaningful question, a progression of tasks, and opportunities for students to develop and defend their own reasoning.

A well-designed investigation may include:

- An introductory phenomenon, puzzle, pattern, or mathematical question.
- Relevant definitions and prerequisites.
- Exploratory examples.
- Accessible starting questions.
- More challenging extensions.
- Opportunities to make conjectures.
- Counterexample-seeking activities.
- Proof or justification tasks.
- Optional computational experiments.
- Reflection questions.
- Facilitator guidance.
- Further questions for independent exploration.

The exact structure should depend on the mathematics being investigated.

National Investigations should give different communities a common mathematical starting point while allowing students to explore different approaches.

### B. Local Labs

Local Labs are community-based groups through which students participate in Math Lab activities.

They may operate in schools, libraries, community centers, or other suitable learning environments.

Local facilitators help organize activities, support discussion, and connect students with investigations and educational resources.

The platform should eventually support Local Labs through features such as:

- Information about the Local Lab model.
- A guide to establishing a Local Lab.
- Facilitator resources.
- Investigation materials.
- Suggested meeting structures.
- Discussion prompts.
- Participation guidance.
- Optional progress and feedback tools.
- Clear contact and onboarding pathways.

Do not invent real Local Labs, facilitators, addresses, memberships, or activity statistics.

If no verified Local Labs have been established, communicate the program as developing and provide a way for interested people to express interest without implying that a network already exists.

### C. Mathematical Learning Resources

Math Lab develops and coordinates resources to support its activities.

These may include:

